text:
The scheme treats the JSON tree as **structure that can be decomposed into independently movable, self-locating fragments**. It does not replace ancestry with a path string. The ancestry remains actual nested JSON inside every fragment. Every keyed object/array container also gets an explicit empty-form element so the container itself survives even if all descendants are separated. fileciteturn91file0L2-L2

There are only six JSON value types to account for: **string, number, boolean, null, object, and array**. The first four are terminal values. Objects and arrays are containers and therefore recurse.

### 1. String

Source:

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "name": "Alice"
  }
}
```

Decomposition:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"name":"Alice"}}
]
```

`"name":"Alice"` is terminal, so it becomes one fundamental element. The complete ancestry needed to locate it—`outer`—remains wrapped around it.

### 2. Number

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "count": 7
  }
}
```

becomes:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"count":7}}
]
```

Nothing special happens because it is numeric. `count:7` is simply one terminal property.

### 3. Boolean

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
```

becomes:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"enabled":true}}
]
```

### 4. Null

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "result": null
  }
}
```

becomes:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"result":null}}
]
```

`null` is still a value. It is not treated as absence.

Those four types therefore have the same decomposition rule:

```text
ancestor structure + one terminal key:value
```

The interesting behavior starts with container values.

---

## 5. Nested object

Source:

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "child": {
      "name": "Alice",
      "age": 30
    }
  }
}
```

Decomposition:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"child_object_literal":{}}},
  {"outer":{"child":{"name":"Alice"}}},
  {"outer":{"child":{"age":30}}}
]
```

Notice the three different informational facts being represented:

```text
outer exists and is an object
outer.child exists and is an object
outer.child.name = "Alice"
outer.child.age = 30
```

The first two are represented by the special `*_object_literal` elements.

The last two are terminal-property fragments.

This is why the scheme can reconstruct structure without relying on any individual leaf to imply that its parent container exists. fileciteturn96file0L7-L7

An **empty object** therefore still survives:

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "child": {}
  }
}
```

becomes:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"child_object_literal":{}}}
]
```

There are no terminal descendants, but the structure is still completely represented.

---

## 6. Nested array

Source:

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "items": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ]
  }
}
```

becomes conceptually:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"items_array_literal":[]}},
  {"outer":{"items":["A"]}},
  {"outer":{"items":["B"]}}
]
```

The array itself gets an empty-form element:

```json
{"outer":{"items_array_literal":[]}}
```

Then **each array member becomes its own projection element**, represented at that array boundary by an array containing exactly one member. fileciteturn91file0L2-L2

So arrays are not decomposed using numeric path metadata such as:

```json
{"path":"outer.items[1]"}
```

Instead, the array syntax itself remains part of the fragment.

An **empty array**:

```json
{
  "outer": {
    "items": []
  }
}
```

has no member elements, but still produces:

```json
[
  {"outer_object_literal":{}},
  {"outer":{"items_array_literal":[]}}
]
```

---

## Array members can themselves be any JSON value

For example:

```json
{
  "values": [
    "A",
    12,
    true,
    null
  ]
}
```

becomes:

```json
[
  {"values_array_literal":[]},
  {"values":["A"]},
  {"values":[12]},
  {"values":[true]},
  {"values":[null]}
]
```

So string, number, boolean and null work identically inside arrays: **one array member per projection element**.

---

## Array containing an object

Now consider:

```json
{
  "people": [
    {
      "name": "Alice",
      "age": 30
    }
  ]
}
```

The recursive principle means the array boundary is retained while the object is decomposed:

```json
[
  {"people_array_literal":[]},

  {"people":[
    {"name":"Alice"}
  ]},

  {"people":[
    {"age":30}
  ]}
]
```

Each fragment says, structurally:

```text
inside people[]
    there is an object
        whose name = Alice
```

or:

```text
inside people[]
    there is an object
        whose age = 30
```

The nested structure is therefore the **signature used to determine which fragments merge**.

This is the point behind the README's phrase **“matching nesting signatures.”**

---

## Object containing an array containing an object

Take:

```json
{
  "group": {
    "people": [
      {
        "name": "Alice",
        "active": true
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

The terminal fragments descend through *all* of the structure:

```json
[
  {"group_object_literal":{}},
  {"group":{"people_array_literal":[]}},

  {"group":{
    "people":[
      {"name":"Alice"}
    ]
  }},

  {"group":{
    "people":[
      {"active":true}
    ]
  }}
]
```

The leaves have been separated, but neither one has lost the fact that it belongs under:

```text
group
  → people array
    → object
```

That nested wrapper is the locator.

---

## Array containing a nested array

The README explicitly applies the same recursion to nested arrays. For:

```json
{
  "outer": [
    {
      "inner": [
        "A",
        "B"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

the documented form is:

```json
[
  {"outer_array_literal":[]},

  {"outer":[
    {"inner_array_literal":[]}
  ]},

  {"outer":[
    {"inner":["A"]}
  ]},

  {"outer":[
    {"inner":["B"]}
  ]}
]
```

So the recursion can continue arbitrarily deeply. fileciteturn92file0L2-L2

---

## The general decomposition rule

For any JSON node:

```text
STRING
NUMBER
BOOLEAN
NULL
    → stop recursion
    → emit one full-route terminal fragment

OBJECT
    → emit its keyed object-literal fragment
    → recurse independently into every property

ARRAY
    → emit its keyed array-literal fragment
    → recurse independently into every member
    → retain an array containing one member at that boundary
```

Thus something arbitrarily complicated like:

```json
{
  "A": {
    "B": [
      {
        "C": {
          "D": [
            {
              "E": false
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

eventually has a terminal element structurally shaped like:

```json
{
  "A": {
    "B": [
      {
        "C": {
          "D": [
            {
              "E": false
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

plus the relevant object/array empty-form elements encountered along that ancestry.

The important idea is therefore **not “remove nesting.”** It is:

> **Separate the informational leaves while copying enough of the original nesting into every element that each leaf remains self-locating.**

Then all of those independent elements can be shuffled at the **outer array only**. Their internal JSON is untouched. Reconstruction consists of finding compatible nesting signatures and recursively merging them back together. fileciteturn91file0L2-L2
